From: Termux packaging
Subject: Use a hyphenated PyPI project name

Termux's generic postinst generator
(scripts/build/termux_step_create_python_debscripts.sh) reads this
package's pip METADATA to pick up its declared runtime dependency on
typing-extensions, and installs it via `pip install <name>` on-device
at package configuration time. To avoid re-downloading/recompiling
pydantic-core itself from PyPI (there's no prebuilt wheel for Android,
and no Rust toolchain on-device), that script skips passing `--upgrade`
when the package's own pip-facing name (with the Termux "python-"
prefix stripped) is already present in the dependency list it's about
to install.

That comparison is a plain string match against the Termux package
name "pydantic-core" (hyphenated) - it does not normalize
underscore/hyphen per PEP 503. Upstream's pyproject.toml declares the
project name with an underscore ('pydantic_core'), so the match fails,
`--upgrade` stays enabled, and the on-device postinst ends up trying
to reinstall/recompile pydantic-core from PyPI, which fails because
there is no Android wheel and no Rust toolchain available there.

Renaming the PyPI-facing project name to use a hyphen fixes the
match. This is purely cosmetic for packaging metadata: maturin's
`module-name` is set explicitly to `pydantic_core._pydantic_core` in
the `[tool.maturin]` section, so `import pydantic_core` is unaffected.

--- a/pydantic-core/pyproject.toml
+++ b/pydantic-core/pyproject.toml
@@ -3,7 +3,7 @@
 build-backend = 'maturin'
 
 [project]
-name = 'pydantic_core'
+name = 'pydantic-core'
 description = "Core functionality for Pydantic validation and serialization"
 requires-python = '>=3.10'
 license = 'MIT'
